---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SectionHead from '../components/SectionHead.astro';
import { site } from '../lib/site';
import { imageSet } from '../lib/url';

const timeline = [
  {
    period: 'Now',
    org: 'Dyson',
    what: 'Consumer insight, business intelligence and CRM across Hong Kong and the region — segmentation, journey design, and the data architecture underneath both.',
  },
  {
    period: 'Earlier',
    org: 'PCCW',
    what: 'Cross-platform user behaviour and content analytics for a subscription business, where retention was the number that mattered.',
  },
  {
    period: 'Earlier',
    org: 'Mattel',
    what: 'Market research and commercial analytics — category landscapes, price-tier share, and the research plan that fed product decisions.',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title={`About — ${site.name}`}
  description="Kenneth Lam — analytics, CRM and AI-assisted engineering in Hong Kong."
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-[76rem] px-5 sm:px-8">
    <section class="grid gap-10 pb-16 pt-16 md:grid-cols-12 md:pt-24">
      <div class="md:col-span-8">
        <p class="kicker">About</p>
        <h1 class="mt-5 text-[clamp(2.4rem,6vw,4.2rem)]">
          Fifteen years of asking what the number actually means.
        </h1>
        <div class="prose-dossier mt-9 text-lg">
          <p>
            I lead analytics work that has to survive contact with a commercial
            team: segmentation that changes who gets contacted, dashboards
            people open on a Monday morning, research that settles an argument
            about which product to launch. At Dyson, PCCW and Mattel that has
            meant doubling a CRM base inside a year, pushing incremental
            repurchase past 20%, and rebuilding the data architecture
            underneath so the market could stop waiting on region for an
            extract.
          </p>
          <p>
            The part that changed recently is the building. For most of my
            career I specified systems and someone else wrote them. Over the
            last year I have been writing them — a trilingual speech
            transcription and summarisation service, an Android app
            instrumented end to end to learn mobile measurement properly, a
            small game shipped to the web, dashboards and graph tooling — with
            an AI pair doing the typing and me doing the deciding.
          </p>
          <p>
            That has made me a better analytics lead, not a worse one. Knowing
            what it costs to emit an event correctly changes how you write a
            tracking spec. Watching a tag configuration produce 1,571 events in
            one session because a trigger was too broad is a different kind of
            education from reading about it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-9 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
          <a
            href={site.resume}
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener"
            class="btn btn-primary"
            data-track="resume_download"
          >
            Download résumé ↗
          </a>
          <a
            href="https://public.tableau.com/app/profile/kenneth.lam/viz/Resume_17168221183140/CV"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener"
            class="btn btn-ghost"
          >
            Résumé as a Tableau viz ↗
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <aside class="md:col-span-4">
        <img
          {...imageSet('/images/avatar.jpg')}
          sizes="(min-width: 768px) 30vw, 100vw"
          alt={`Portrait of ${site.name}`}
          width="1024"
          height="1024"
          class="aspect-square w-full rounded-full border-[6px] border-surface object-cover shadow-[0_24px_50px_-28px_rgb(30_20_12/0.45)]"
        />
      </aside>
    </section>

    <section class="pb-20">
      <SectionHead num="01" label="Where" title="Career" />
      <dl class="mt-10 border-t border-rule">
        {
          timeline.map((t) => (
            <div class="grid gap-2 border-b border-rule py-6 md:grid-cols-12 md:gap-6">
              <dt class="kicker pt-1 md:col-span-2">{t.period}</dt>
              <dd class="md:col-span-10">
                <p class="font-display text-3xl leading-none">{t.org}</p>
                <p class="mt-3 max-w-[62ch] text-fg-muted">{t.what}</p>
              </dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="pb-24">
      <SectionHead num="02" label="How" title="How I work" />
      <div class="mt-10 grid gap-10 md:grid-cols-3">
        <div class="panel p-6">
          <h3 class="kicker">Start from the decision</h3>
          <p class="mt-4 text-fg-muted">
            A metric nobody would act on differently is a metric not worth
            building. I would rather ship three numbers a team uses than thirty
            it admires.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel p-6">
          <h3 class="kicker">Instrument before you analyse</h3>
          <p class="mt-4 text-fg-muted">
            Most analytics problems are collection problems wearing a costume.
            Tracking specs, naming conventions and validation belong at the
            start, not after the first report disappoints.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel p-6">
          <h3 class="kicker">Build the small version</h3>
          <p class="mt-4 text-fg-muted">
            A working prototype settles arguments that a document extends.
            Certified Professional Scrum Master, which mostly means I know when
            the ceremony is helping and when it is not.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
